// Practice quiz: Handling HTTP errors and ProblemDetail responses
// First draft: unreviewed. Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: html-css-js/fetch-and-browser-client/handling-http-errors-and-problemdetail-responses

::o1 which failure::Code catches a TypeError thrown by await fetch(url). What can you conclude? {
~The server answered 500 #Not quite. That would fulfill with ok false.
~The JSON was malformed #Not quite. That would reject res.json(), not fetch.
=No usable response reached your code, from a network failure or a CORS block #Correct. fetch rejects on network errors, and a CORS block counts as one.
~The server answered 404 #Not quite. That would fulfill with ok false.
}

::o1 no status::In the catch block for a rejected fetch, what can you read about the HTTP status? {
~err.status holds it #Not quite. A rejected fetch gives a plain error with no status.
~It is always 0 #Not quite. No response object exists to report one.
=Nothing, because no response exists #Correct. There is no status to read.
~It is always 500 #Not quite. The server may be unreachable; no status was sent.
}

::o2 detail member::In a problem details body, what does the detail member hold? {
~The HTTP version used #Not quite. That is not a problem member.
~A stack trace by definition #Not quite. Stack traces should not be exposed to clients.
~The URL of the request only #Not quite. That is closer to instance.
=An explanation specific to this occurrence of the problem #Correct. title gives the general kind, and detail gives this case.
}

::o2 media type::Which media type identifies an RFC 9457 JSON problem response? {
~application/error+json #Not quite. That is not the registered type.
=application/problem+json #Correct. That is the registered JSON form.
~text/problem #Not quite. That is not the registered type.
~application/json; problem #Not quite. It has its own media type rather than a parameter.
}

::o3 forbidden::A signed-in user receives 403. Which message fits best? {
=You do not have permission to do this #Correct. The server knows who they are, and refuses.
~Please sign in #Not quite. That fits 401; they are already signed in.
~Check your internet connection #Not quite. A response did arrive.
~The page has moved #Not quite. That fits a redirect.
}
